      "math_academy_id": "e-5142",
      "problem": "Solve $f(x)=0$, where $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^3-2x^2-9x+18}{x^2-x-2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-9x+18}{{x}^{2}-x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(x-2)-9(x-2)}{(x-2)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)({x}^{2}-9)}{(x-2)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x-3)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-3)(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the numerator equal to zero and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+3) = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\pm3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(x)=0$ are $x=3$ and $x=-3.$",
